<sn><md>### LAN

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/lan_diagram.jpeg)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
Local Area Network
- **Range:** Up to ~1 km (a building or campus)
- **Purpose:** Connects devices within a limited area
- **Examples:** Home Wi-Fi, office network, school computer lab
- **Speed:** High (100 Mbps – 10 Gbps)
- **Owner:** Private (home, business)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### WLAN

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
Wireless Local Area Network
- **Range:** Similar to LAN (~100 m per access point)
- **Purpose:** A LAN that uses wireless (Wi-Fi) instead of cables
- **Examples:** Home Wi-Fi, café hotspots, airport Wi-Fi
- **Note:** A subset of LAN — same scope, wireless medium
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/wlan_diagram.jpeg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### CAN

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/can_diagram.jpeg)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
Campus Area Network
- **Range:** ~1–5 km (multiple buildings)
- **Purpose:** Connects several LANs within a campus
- **Examples:** University campus, corporate headquarters, military base
- **Note:** Larger than LAN, smaller than MAN
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### MAN

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
Metropolitan Area Network
- **Range:** ~5–50 km (a city)
- **Purpose:** Connects LANs across a city
- **Examples:** City-wide cable TV network, municipal broadband, bank branches across a city
- **Owner:** Usually a large organization or ISP
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/man_diagram.jpeg)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### WAN

:::.row
:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
![img](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Nh-em0n/QDoc/main/BCS/Computer%20and%20ICT/B.Computer%20Networks/assets/wan_diagram.jpeg)
:::

:::.col-12 .col-lg-6
Wide Area Network
- **Range:** Country, continent, or global
- **Purpose:** Connects MANs/LANs over large distances
- **Examples:** The Internet, corporate networks spanning countries, 4G/5G networks
- **Owner:** Multiple organizations, telecom providers
- **Speed:** Generally lower than LAN (due to distance)
:::
:::
</md></sn><sn><md>### Quick Comparison Table

| Type | Full Name | Range | Example |
|------|-----------|-------|---------|
| PAN | Personal Area Network | ~10 m | Bluetooth |
| LAN | Local Area Network | ~1 km | Office network |
| WLAN | Wireless LAN | ~100 m | Wi-Fi |
| CAN | Campus Area Network | ~5 km | University |
| MAN | Metropolitan Area Network | ~50 km | City cable |
| WAN | Wide Area Network | Global | Internet |

+++ ...
![img](https://ipcisco.com/wp-content/uploads/2022/03/types-of-networks-ipcisco.png)
+++

**Key idea:** The main differences are **geographic scope**, **ownership**, and **transmission medium** (wired vs. wireless). Networks can also nest — e.g., a WAN contains MANs, which contain LANs, which may contain PANs.

+++ ...
